<Seo Canonical="/docs/helpers/utilities/z-index" Title="Blazorise Z-Index Utility" Description="Choose stacking layers for sticky headers, floating toolbars, and custom overlays with the Blazorise Z-Index utility." />

<DocsPageTitle Path="Helpers/Utilities/Z-Index">
    Blazorise Z-Index Utility
</DocsPageTitle>

<DocsPageLead>
    Control which elements appear in front of others, from sticky headers and floating toolbars to custom overlays.
</DocsPageLead>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Layers
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Choose a layer that matches your use case, and Blazorise selects the appropriate stacking value.
    Available layers are <Code>ZIndex.Dropdown</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Sticky</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Fixed</Code>,
    <Code>ZIndex.OffcanvasBackdrop</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Offcanvas</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.ModalBackdrop</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Modal</Code>,
    <Code>ZIndex.Popover</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Tooltip</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Toast</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Snackbar</Code>, or <Code>ZIndex.OnScreenKeyboard</Code>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    For a floating toolbar, use <Code>&lt;Div Position="Position.Fixed" ZIndex="ZIndex.Fixed" /&gt;</Code>.
    Use <Code>Position</Code> to control placement and <Code>ZIndex</Code> to control stacking order.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Named">
        <Paragraph>
            Use named layers to match an element's role.
            Here, <Code>ZIndex.Modal</Code> places the modal layer in front of <Code>ZIndex.Fixed</Code>.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ZIndexExample" Outlined>
        <ZIndexExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Levels">
        <Paragraph>
            Use <Code>ZIndex.IsNegative1</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Is0</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Is1</Code>, <Code>ZIndex.Is2</Code>, and <Code>ZIndex.Is3</Code>
            to arrange overlapping elements. Blazorise selects the appropriate value for each level.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            Higher levels appear in front of lower levels. In this example, each block appears in front of the next one.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ZIndexLevelsExample" Outlined>
        <ZIndexLevelsExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Numeric">
        <Paragraph>
            Use a number when you need an exact value, for example <Code>&lt;Div ZIndex="2000" /&gt;</Code>.
            Both <Code>int</Code> and <Code>int?</Code> values are supported, including zero and negative numbers.
        </Paragraph>
        <Paragraph>
            These blocks use values from 3 down to -1. Higher values appear in front of lower values.
        </Paragraph>
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="ZIndexNumericExample" Outlined>
        <ZIndexNumericExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Usage
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use <Code>UtilityTarget</Code> to choose whether <Code>ZIndex</Code> applies to a component or its wrapper, where supported.
    It follows the same target as the component's other utilities.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    A parent's stacking order can limit which elements its children can overlap.
    If increasing a child's level does not bring it forward, check the parent's stacking order too.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Defaults
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Set <Code>ZIndex.Default</Code> or pass <Code>null</Code> to restore the component's default stacking behavior.
    Use <Code>ZIndex.Is0</Code> when you want to explicitly choose the zero level.
</DocsPageParagraph>